<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<ContentView
    x:Class="LoanCalculatorMaui.Controls.InfoHintView"
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
    x:Name="Root"
    Margin="0,-9,0,-3">

    <!--
        An inline "ⓘ <prompt>" strip that expands to an explanation, for dropping in directly
        beneath the thing it explains.

        Why this shape rather than one explanation card per screen: a single card has to sit
        somewhere, and wherever it sits it is away from most of what it describes. On a screen made
        of several unrelated controls that means a wall of text at the bottom that most people
        never scroll to. One of these per control puts each explanation where the question is
        actually asked.

        Why the prompt text and not a bare glyph: a lone ⓘ gives no reason to tap it. The prompt
        says what you would get, and it also makes the tap target the width of a sentence instead of
        the width of one character.

        Not an SfExpander, deliberately. Expander headers are the Syncfusion control that swallows
        taps on iOS unless the hosting page calls SyncfusionIosTouchFix.ApplyToExpanders, and it
        fails silently when it is missed — see CLAUDE.md. A TapGestureRecognizer on a Label has none
        of that fragility, which is also why SwitchWithInfoView and the Budget summary heading use
        the same approach.
    -->
    <!--
        The negative vertical margin on the root above is deliberate — please do not "tidy" it
        away.

        Every host places one of these inside a VerticalStackLayout with Spacing="12", so the hint
        was being given 12pt above AND 12pt below on top of its own padding: about 40pt of air
        around a single line of text, which left the ⓘ floating between the thing it explains and
        the next control rather than belonging to either. The negative margin absorbs most of that
        spacing without touching the parent's Spacing, which every other row on those screens
        depends on.

        Asymmetric on purpose (-9 top, -3 bottom): the hint explains the element ABOVE it, so it
        should sit tight under that (about 3pt) and leave a clearer gap (about 9pt) before the next
        control. Grouping it with its own subject is what makes it readable at a glance.

        The padding on the Label below is NOT part of the gap problem and stays: it is the tap
        target. Trimming it would make the line hard to hit, which is why the fix is a margin
        rather than less padding.
    -->
    <VerticalStackLayout Spacing="0">

        <!--
            Padding is the tap target. The glyph and the prompt are one Label so the whole line is
            tappable, rather than only the character.
        -->
        <Label
            Padding="0,6"
            AutomationId="{Binding InfoAutomationId, Source={x:Reference Root}}"
            FontSize="{StaticResource FontSizeSmall}"
            TextColor="{DynamicResource LoanAppTabIndicatorStrokeColor}">
            <Label.FormattedText>
                <FormattedString>
                    <Span Text="ⓘ  " />
                    <Span Text="{Binding Prompt, Source={x:Reference Root}}" />
                </FormattedString>
            </Label.FormattedText>
            <Label.GestureRecognizers>
                <TapGestureRecognizer Tapped="OnHintTapped" />
            </Label.GestureRecognizers>
        </Label>

        <Label
            x:Name="DetailLabel"
            Margin="0,0,0,12"
            AutomationId="{Binding DetailAutomationId, Source={x:Reference Root}}"
            FontSize="{StaticResource FontSizeSmall}"
            IsVisible="False"
            Opacity="0.8"
            Text="{Binding Detail, Source={x:Reference Root}}"
            TextColor="{DynamicResource LoanAppDefaultForegroundColor}" />

        <!--
            The same explanation in the reader's own numbers, when the host supplies one. Bold
            rather than muted: the general text says what something means, this says what it means
            for you, which is the part people actually want.
        -->
        <Label
            x:Name="LiveExampleLabel"
            Margin="0,0,0,14"
            FontAttributes="Bold"
            FontSize="{StaticResource FontSizeSmall}"
            IsVisible="False"
            Text="{Binding LiveExample, Source={x:Reference Root}}"
            TextColor="{DynamicResource LoanAppDefaultForegroundColor}" />

    </VerticalStackLayout>

</ContentView>
